# ── Browser ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything prefixed VITE_ is inlined into the bundle and readable in devtools.
# That is correct for the publishable key: it identifies the project and
# authorises nothing on its own, because the row level security policies in
# supabase/01-schema.sql decide who may read which row.
#
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# Older projects issue a JWT anon key instead; that name still works:
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# ── Server only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None of these are ever prefixed VITE_, and none reach the browser.

# Drafting. Without it the AI step degrades to "write it yourself".
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Stock photo search (21.2). Each is optional; the picker offers only the
# providers with a key, and with neither it says so instead of searching.
# The keys stay here: the browser searches through /api/stock.
# Unsplash: https://unsplash.com/oauth/applications, the "Access Key".
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=
# Pexels: https://www.pexels.com/api/, "Your API Key".
PEXELS_API_KEY=

# Reads and writes profiles.plan. This key BYPASSES row level security
# completely, it is the one thing that can grant a plan, which is exactly why
# the browser must never see it.
# Dashboard, Project Settings, API Keys, "Create secret key" (sb_secret_...).
# The legacy service_role JWT still works and is read as a fallback, but
# Supabase is deprecating it by the end of 2026.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# From the same screen, "New client-side token". This one is PUBLIC: it is
# handed to the browser so Paddle's checkout can open, and it can do nothing
# except that. It still lives here rather than in a VITE_ variable, so every
# piece of billing configuration is in one file on one machine.
# Developer tools, Notifications, your destination, "Secret key" (pdl_ntfset_).
# Not the API key. Without it the webhook route refuses every request, which is
# the right default: an unverified webhook is an open door to a free
# subscription.
# PRICE ids (pri_...), not product ids. A product holds prices (monthly,
# yearly) and a subscription item carries the price. Catalog, Products, open
# the product, and the price id is on each price. `npm run paddle:prices`
# prints them all.

# Where the checkout sends people back to when payment succeeds. It must also
# be an approved domain in Paddle, under Checkout, Website approval.
PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ── Video export (Batch 26) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The system's ffmpeg is used; it is not bundled. Leave blank to use PATH.
# /api/health reports `video: false` when none is found.
FFMPEG_PATH=
# Where the video queue keeps its jobs. Put it on a volume that outlives a
# deploy, or an interrupted render is reported failed instead of resumed.
VIDEO_DIR=
# Renders at once on this node (each is a Chromium page and an x264 encode).
VIDEO_WORKERS=1
# How long a deploy's SIGTERM waits for the render in hand, in milliseconds.
VIDEO_GRACE_MS=25000
